# GLP / OECD Guidelines — DABT Reference

## Historical Context (Why GLP exists)

**The IBT scandal (1976):** Industrial Bio-Test Laboratories, one of the largest US contract testing labs, was found to have systematically falsified nonclinical safety data across hundreds of studies supporting hundreds of products. FDA auditors discovered:
- Study directors who never visited animal rooms
- Necropsy dates falling on weekends with no weekend staff on record
- Tumors reported in animals that didn't have them on reinspection
- Entire studies fabricated — animals never ordered, reports issued anyway

**Consequence:** The FDA promulgated 21 CFR Part 58 (1978), creating Good Laboratory Practice regulations. Other countries followed: OECD GLP Principles (1981), Japan's MAP/MHW GLP, UK GLP. All share the same core philosophy: **what isn't written down didn't happen.**

---

## The 10 Principles of GLP (OECD Series on Principles of GLP, ENV/MC/CHEM(98)17)

### 1. Resources — Organization and Personnel
- **Study Director:** Single point of control — one person responsible for the overall conduct of the study and interpretation of results. MUST be named in the protocol. Cannot delegate this role.
- **QA Unit (Quality Assurance):** Independent from study personnel. Reads protocols, inspects facilities (regularly), audits data and reports. Reports to management, not to the study director.
- **Management:** Provides resources, ensures GLP compliance, appoints Study Director, maintains facility.
- **Archivist:** Controls the archive (raw data, reports, specimens).

### 2. Resources — Facilities
- Separate areas for: receipt/storage of test/reference items, animal housing, diet/padding storage, surgery, necropsy, clinical pathology, data storage.
- Environmental controls (temp, humidity, light cycles) monitored and recorded.
- Quarantine for new animals before study start.

### 3. Resources — Care, Housing, and Feeding of Test Animals
- Separation by species, study, health status.
- Identified individually (cage cards, ear tags, microchips, tattoo).
- Environmental enrichment (where it doesn't confound endpoints).
- Feed/water analyzed for contaminants that could interfere with the study.

### 4. Resources — Apparatus, Materials, Reagents
- All equipment: qualified, calibrated, maintained — with written records.
- Computers/systems: validated, secure, with audit trails.
- Cleaning/sanitization schedules documented.

### 5. Rules — Test Systems
- **Animal studies:** species, strain, source, age, weight range documented. Acclimation period before dosing. Health monitoring.
- **In vivo:** Dose route, frequency, formulation analysis (concentration, stability, homogeneity).
- **In vitro:** Cell line identity/passage, culture conditions, contamination checks.

### 6. Rules — Test and Reference Items
- Characterized (identity, purity, composition, stability).
- Homogeneity and stability of formulations verified.
- Retained samples for each batch.

### 7. Rules — Standard Operating Procedures (SOPs)
- ALL routine procedures must have SOPs: animal care, dosing, necropsy, histopathology, data handling, equipment use, cleaning, QA audits.
- SOPs are approved, dated, controlled (current version in use, obsolete versions archived).
- Deviations from SOPs must be documented and authorized.

### 8. Rules — Performance of the Study (the Protocol)
- The **protocol** is written before the study starts. Contains: study title/number, sponsor, testing facility, study director, proposed dates, test/reference items, test system, dose levels, route, schedule, endpoints, statistical methods.
- **Amendments** — formal written changes to the protocol after it's signed.
- **Deviations** — unplanned departures from the protocol that occur during execution. Documented in raw data and in the final report.

### 9. Rules — Reporting of Study Results
- The **final report** must include: study identification, name of study director + names of other scientists involved, location, test/reference items, test system, dosing regimen, results (tables, figures), statistical analyses, study director's signed QA statement, dates, archive location.
- All raw data, specimens, and records must be archived after report completion.
- **GLP compliance statement** — the final report states whether the study was conducted in compliance with GLP. If not (e.g., exploratory work under GLP-like conditions), this must be explicitly stated.

### 10. Rules — Storage and Retention of Records and Materials
- **Archive** — secure, fire-resistant, access-controlled, environmentally managed.
- Retention periods: at least 10-15 years for most records (varies by jurisdiction).
- What goes to archive: raw data, processed data, final report, SOPs, protocol + amendments, specimens (where feasible), QA records, equipment maintenance records.
- **Raw data** — any original record: handwritten lab notebooks, instrument printouts, automated data collection files, specimen labels, photographic records, signed observations.
- **Electronic records** — 21 CFR Part 11 compliance (audit trail, authorized access, backup, data integrity).

